WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.080
In 1923, X-rays of one known wavelength
were aimed at a block of graphite.

2
00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.390
A detector swung around to measure the
wavelength of the X-rays scattered at different

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.530
angles.

4
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.000
Some came out with a longer wavelength.

5
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.880
And the wider the angle,
the bigger the shift.

6
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.670
Wave theory predicted the same
wavelength at every angle.

7
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.400
So why would light come out stretched?

8
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:26.630
In this mission, you'll chart
the shift at every angle,

9
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.320
from 0 to 180 degrees.

10
00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.290
Then you'll find out why
this shows up with X-rays,

11
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.710
but is far too small to
measure with visible light.

12
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.590
Your mission: explain why scattered
X-rays come out with a longer wavelength.
